From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Jon Pretty <jon.pretty@propensive.com>
Date: Wed, 7 Oct 2026 17:06:48 +0200
Subject: A shared, level-exempt classifier: caps.SharedUnscoped
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Squashed from 2 commits, oldest first:

* Add caps.SharedUnscoped, a shared and level-exempt classifier

`Unscoped` exempts a capability from the level check — a value minted at a
deeper level may flow into a result capture set minted further out — but it
extends `ExclusiveCapability`, so no shared capability can carry it. An
ambient effect strategy that is aliased freely (a `SharedCapability`, so that
a codec capturing it and a call taking it as evidence are not a separation
overlap) and minted by a polymorphic given at its use site needs both.

`SharedUnscoped extends SharedCapability, Classifier` is that point in the
lattice. The compiler's five consultations of `Unscoped` go through one
predicate, `isUnscopedClassifier`, which recognises either; exclusivity is
untouched, so separation checking treats a `SharedUnscoped` capability as any
shared one.

Written with LLM assistance (Claude Code); reviewed content.

* Ship SharedUnscoped in proscala-library, looked up leniently

The trait moves from `scala/caps/package.scala`, which must stay
byte-identical to upstream's `scala-library`, into the supplementary
`proscala-library` jar beside `Spreadable` and `Literate`. The compiler
resolves it with `getClassIfDefined`, as it does those two, so a
classpath without the jar simply has no capability classified this way
and nothing fails to load.

Written with LLM assistance (Claude Code); reviewed content.

